%T A398005 34428483,130948812,497726208,1890808029,7179951618,27255325662,
%U A398005 103434972003,392458313484,1488842117928,5647388078637,21419150942898,81230998124214,308044590380163,1168109195049612,4429308644898768
%N A398005 Number of domino towers in 3 dimensions consisting of n blocks 2 X 1 X 1, connected and supported, counted up to horizontal translation.
%C A398005 A domino tower in 3 dimensions is a connected configuration of n blocks of size 2 X 1 X 1 on the cubic lattice Z^2 X Z_{>=0}, where each block is supported (rests on the ground or on another block), counted up to horizontal translation. This is the 3-dimensional generalization of Example 21 in Section 1.3.2.2 of the Handbook of Enumerative Combinatorics (Bona, 2015), where the 2-dimensional case gives 3^(n-1) towers.
%C A398005 The characteristic polynomial factors as (x - 3)*(x^2 - 3*x - 3), with dominant root (3 + sqrt(21))/2 = A090458.
%D A398005 M. Bóna (ed.), Handbook of Enumerative Combinatorics, CRC Press / Chapman & Hall, 2015, Example 21, Section 1.3.2.2, pp. 46-48.
%H A398005 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (6,-6,-9).
%F A398005 a(n) = 6*a(n-1) - 6*a(n-2) - 9*a(n-3) for n >= 4, with a(1) = 3, a(2) = 12, a(3) = 48.
%F A398005 G.f.: 3*x*(1 - 2*x - 2*x^2)/((1 - 3*x)*(1 - 3*x - 3*x^2)).
%e A398005 For n=1, there are a(1) = 3 towers consisting of a single block (three possible orientations).
%e A398005 For n=2, there are a(2) = 12 towers consisting of two blocks.
%e A398005 For n=3, there are a(3) = 48 towers consisting of three blocks.
%o A398005 (Python)
%o A398005 def A_list(N):
%o A398005     seq = [3, 12, 48]
%o A398005     for n in range(4, N+1):
%o A398005         seq.append(6*seq[-1] - 6*seq[-2] - 9*seq[-3])
%o A398005     return seq[:N]
%Y A398005 Cf. A000244 (2-dimensional analog), A090458.
%K A398005 nonn,easy,new
%O A398005 1,1
%A A398005 _Jean Lauro Muller_, Aug 14 2026
